proxmox-csi-storage (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Set `proxyto => 'node'` on `copy` and `rename`. Without it PVE never
    forwards the request to the node named in the path, so both methods ran on
    whichever host received it — for proxmox-csi-plugin, always the single host
    its cluster URL points at. With node-local storage that made every volume on
    every other node invisible: `rename` failed with
    "500 source volume 'local:9999/vm-9999-pvc-<uuid>.raw' not found" for
    volumes that plainly existed, seconds after they were created, and
    `features.reassignVolumeOnAttach` was silently inert everywhere except that
    one host. Attaches were unaffected — the driver treats a failed rename as
    non-fatal — and no volume needs repairing: each is renamed correctly on its
    next attach.
  * `copy`'s `target_node` now defaults to the request node, as its description
    always claimed. It previously defaulted to whichever host answered. No
    caller was affected; proxmox-csi-plugin always sends it explicitly.

 -- CrunchyMonkies <opensource@crunchymonkies.invalid>  Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

proxmox-csi-storage (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add a `rename` endpoint, wrapping PVE::Storage::rename_volume. It is the
    only mechanism PVE has for reassigning an *unattached* volume to another
    vmid, and it has no REST endpoint of its own — which is why
    proxmox-csi-plugin's `features.reassignVolumeOnAttach` could not work
    against move_disk (that method moves a disk key between two REAL VMs, and
    the CSI placeholder vmid 9999 is not a VM). Synchronous: a rename is an
    in-directory rename(2) on dir/LVM/ZFS, so there is no worker to fork.
  * rename_volume performs no in-use check of its own, so the endpoint supplies
    one: it walks the cluster vmlist and refuses if any qemu or lxc config
    references the source volume. A pure `unused<n>` reference is tolerated —
    that is the residue the CSI driver's own detach leaves behind, and it
    removes it after the rename — but an attached disk, a pending change or a
    snapshot reference is refused. Failure to read any config is fatal, so the
    scan fails closed.
  * ACLs match what move_disk itself enforces, so no new privilege class is
    introduced: Datastore.Audit (declared) plus, in code, check_volume_access on
    the source, Datastore.AllocateSpace on the storage, and VM.Config.Disk on
    /vms/<target_vmid>.
  * The rename target volname is validated by a stricter allow-list than the
    source: PVE::Storage::Plugin::rename_volume composes the destination path
    itself as "<basedir>/<target_vmid>/<target_volname>", so the '<vmid>/' shape
    that is legitimate for a source volname would be a traversal here.

 -- CrunchyMonkies <opensource@crunchymonkies.invalid>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

proxmox-csi-storage (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Accept directory-plugin volnames. The volname allow-list applied to both
    the source `volume` parameter and the target volname rejected '/', but
    file-backed storages name every volume '<vmid>/<name>.<ext>' — so
    directory-storage copies were rejected outright, and proxmox-csi-plugin's
    snapshot path (which always produces that shape when the disk has a file
    extension) could not use this endpoint at all. The pattern now admits one
    optional leading '<vmid>/' component, where <vmid> is PVE's VMID range and
    nothing else; ':', '..', absolute paths and deeper nesting stay rejected.
    maxLength raised 128 -> 160 to fit the prefix.

 -- CrunchyMonkies <opensource@crunchymonkies.invalid>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

proxmox-csi-storage (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release: token-authorised copy endpoint for proxmox-csi-plugin,
    as a proxmod extension. Supersedes the pve-csi-copy systemd-wrapper hack.

